<article class="post-body">

<!-- §01 -->
<section id="s-intro" data-code="intro">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Iteration Gap</div>
<h2>Why a single forward pass isn't enough</h2>
<p>
  Masked Diffusion Models (MDMs) generate text by iteratively unmasking tokens over many denoising steps. At each step, the transformer runs a forward pass and predicts every masked position <em>simultaneously</em>. This parallelism is powerful — but it has a hidden limitation.
</p>
<p>
  Within a single forward pass, masked positions can't revise each other. Once the network computes a hidden state for position 3, that hidden state can't incorporate what position 7 "figured out" — the two positions reason from whatever they learned in the previous step. For short, locally-constrained text this is fine. For globally-constrained problems — Sudoku, arithmetic, multi-hop reasoning — it's a bottleneck.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">single-step vs. looped prediction</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to switch mode</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="intro-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem;margin-top:.75rem;justify-content:center">
      <button class="sudoku-btn active" id="intro-btn-single" onclick="setIntroMode('single')">S = 1 (no loops)</button>
      <button class="sudoku-btn" id="intro-btn-loop" onclick="setIntroMode('loop')">S = 12 (with loops)</button>
    </div>
    <p style="text-align:center;font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin:.5rem 0 0;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">
      "The cat ___ on the ___ near ___ Paris" — globally constrained prediction
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Looped architectures solve this by applying a <em>shared</em> transformer block S times per step. Each loop pass can incorporate the refined predictions of the previous pass, letting masked positions gradually converge on a globally consistent answer.
</p>
<p>
  LoopMDM (Lee et al., 2026) is the first systematic application of looped transformers to masked diffusion language models, and the results are striking: 3.3× fewer training FLOPs to reach the same quality, +8.5 points on GSM8K math, at zero additional parameter cost.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 -->
<section id="s-background" data-code="background">
<div class="section-no">02 — MDM Primer</div>
<h2>Masked Diffusion Language Models</h2>
<p>
  Before diving into the loop, here's the MDM setup. Given a clean token sequence x₀, the <strong>forward process</strong> randomly replaces each token with a special [MASK] token with probability (1 − αₜ) at noise level t. At t = 1, everything is masked; at t = 0, the text is clean.
</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Forward masking process</div>
  q(<span class="math-var">xᵢₜ</span> | <span class="math-var">xᵢ₀</span>) = <span class="math-var">αₜ</span> · δ(<span class="math-var">xᵢ₀</span>) + (1 − <span class="math-var">αₜ</span>) · δ([MASK])
  <div class="math-comment">  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;← keeps token with prob αₜ, masks with prob (1 − αₜ)</div>
</div>
<p>
  The <strong>reverse process</strong> starts from a fully-masked sequence and iteratively unmasks. At each step, the denoising network pθ predicts the clean token at each masked position, and some masked tokens are "committed" to their predicted value. After T steps, all masks are resolved and we have generated text.
</p>
<p>
  The training objective is the <strong>negative ELBO (NELBO)</strong> — a sum over masked positions, weighted by the noise schedule derivative α′ₜ/(1 − αₜ):
</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">NELBO training loss</div>
  ℒ = 𝔼<sub>t,xₜ</sub> [∑<sub>i</sub> I[<span class="math-var">xᵢₜ</span> = MASK] · <span class="math-var">αₜ′</span>/(1 − <span class="math-var">αₜ</span>) · log &lt;p<sub>θ</sub>(<span class="math-var">x₀ᵢ</span> | xₜ, t), <span class="math-var">x₀ᵢ</span>&gt;]
  <div class="math-comment">  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;← only penalizes predictions at masked positions</div>
</div>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">progressive unmasking animation</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">watch the denoising steps</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="unmask-canvas" style="width:100%;height:140px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem;margin-top:.75rem;justify-content:center">
      <button class="sudoku-btn active" id="unmask-play-btn" onclick="startUnmask()">▶ Play</button>
      <button class="sudoku-btn" onclick="resetUnmask()">↺ Reset</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The critical design challenge: what should the <em>architecture</em> of pθ look like? Prior work focused on training objectives and noise schedules; LoopMDM addresses the architecture directly, asking: can repeated computation within a single denoising step improve quality?
</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 -->
<section id="s-arch" data-code="arch">
<div class="section-no">03 — LoopMDM Architecture</div>
<h2>Shared mid-block, repeated S times</h2>
<p>
  LoopMDM divides the denoising transformer into three groups: a <strong>head</strong> of nₕ layers (run once, builds initial representations), a <strong>mid-block</strong> of n_m layers with <em>shared weights applied S times in a loop</em>, and a <strong>tail</strong> that produces the final token distributions.
</p>

