<!-- §04 -->
<section id="s-workspace" data-code="workspace">
<div class="section-no">04 — Masks as a Parallel Workspace</div>
<h2>How loops let masked tokens reason together</h2>
<p>
  Why does looping help at all? The key insight: <strong>masked positions act as a parallel scratchpad</strong>. In each loop pass, masked tokens can incorporate what other masked tokens are "thinking" — their current hidden states. Repeated loops let this information propagate across the entire masked sequence before committing to predictions.
</p>
<p>
  The Sudoku puzzle below makes this concrete. MDMs can normally solve Sudoku by choosing easy cells first (adaptive unmasking). LoopMDM researchers disabled that advantage by enforcing <em>left-to-right generation</em> — identical to how autoregressive models work — so any improvement must come purely from within-step reasoning. Under this constraint, a 1-layer model without looping gets only 10.9% accuracy. With loops, errors shrink step by step:
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">sudoku: loop count vs. errors (left-to-right generation order)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click S buttons to see errors decrease</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="sudoku-btns">
      <button class="sudoku-btn active" id="sb1" onclick="setSudokuS(1)">S = 1 &nbsp;(24 err)</button>
      <button class="sudoku-btn" id="sb2" onclick="setSudokuS(2)">S = 2 &nbsp;(7 err)</button>
      <button class="sudoku-btn" id="sb3" onclick="setSudokuS(3)">S = 3 &nbsp;(0 err)</button>
    </div>
    <div class="sudoku-wrap">
      <div class="sudoku-grid" id="sudoku-grid"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="sudoku-error-count" id="sudoku-ec">
      <span style="color:#f87171;font-weight:700">24 errors</span> — locally plausible guesses, globally inconsistent
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  After loop 1, the model's predictions are locally reasonable but violate Sudoku constraints globally — "sat" and "mat" both look fine from their own context, but collide row-wise. Loop 2 can see the states from loop 1 at every masked position simultaneously, spot the collision, and revise. Loop 3 achieves a globally consistent solution with zero errors.
</p>

<h3>Mask-to-mask attention grows with loop count</h3>
<p>
  The mechanism behind this: as loop count S increases, the fraction of attention mass flowing from masked positions to <em>other</em> masked positions grows steadily. At S = 1 (equivalent to a plain MDM forward pass), each masked position mostly attends to unmasked context. At S = 12, masked positions spend significantly more attention on each other — building up a shared reasoning space.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">mask-to-mask attention vs. loop count</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">attention among masked positions increases with S</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="attn-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The attention saturates near S_max = 12, consistent with the training distribution. Even beyond S_max = 12, the trend holds — suggesting the loop operator has learned a general refinement procedure, not one that overfits to a specific loop count.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 -->
<section id="s-selective" data-code="selective">
<div class="section-no">05 — Where &amp; How Much to Loop</div>
<h2>Selective placement is critical — not all layers are equal</h2>
<p>
  Looping every layer sounds like the obvious choice, but it's actually counterproductive. The paper's ablation (Table 4) shows a clear pattern: the loop must be placed at <strong>early-middle layers</strong>, applied to a <strong>small number of layers</strong>, with a <strong>moderate maximum loop count</strong>. Deviating in any direction hurts.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">NLL vs. loop position (lower is better; baseline = 3.798)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">positions 1–2 are the sweet spot</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="pos-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Positions 0 (closest to the input embedding) and 9–10 (closest to the output head) both underperform. The sweet spot at positions 1–2 captures the gains: the head has already built basic token representations, but the tail hasn't yet specialized for final prediction. Repeated computation in this "middle" zone is most effective.
</p>

<h3>How many looped layers? Two is enough.</h3>
<p>
  Adding more layers to the looped block is also counterproductive. With n_m = 2 mid-layers and S_max = 12 loops, the effective depth is 24 extra "virtual layers" — far more than adding 2 real layers. But looping 6+ layers underperforms the baseline, because each loop is more expensive and the fixed FLOP budget buys fewer training steps.
</p>

<h3>Adaptive loops: 12 → 5.1 average, same performance</h3>
<p>
  Not every denoising timestep needs the full S = 12 loops. The NLL gain from additional loops peaks at intermediate noise levels (where the model has both context and unresolved positions) and is small near t = 0 (nearly clean) or t = 1 (nearly fully masked).
</p>
<p>
  The paper proposes a simple adaptive rule: monitor the relative hidden-state change between loop iterations. If ‖H⁽ᵏ⁾ − H⁽ᵏ⁻¹⁾‖/‖H⁽ᵏ⁾‖ falls below ε = 0.1, stop early. This reduces the average loop count from 12 to just 5.1 while preserving essentially the same performance.
</p>

